// crowd of beetles, dragonfly, Pangolin watching Hornbill yap
Narrator: On your way to the beach, you notice a gathering of creatures in the forest, enraptured by a performance ‘onstage.’
Hornbill: And so, as the hero Noah stopped the flood, many creatures and humans did not have to hide in the mountains, as they would normally do.
Beetler: But why is the kraken so evil?
Beetlette: Yeah! Destroying world!
Hornbill: Oh, like many things, we cannot fully guess its purpose.
Hornbill: Why does the Sun only rise after the Moon? And what makes seeds bloom into flowers and trees?
Hornbill: Sometimes, that's just the way of things. But other times — those like Noah are able to try and change their fate.
Hornbill: In this case, success didn't matter. For even if he ‘failed,’ we still tell his story.
Hornbill: After all, not everyone can survive a storm such as the Great Disaster. A human against that, is like a beetle to a monsteratops! 
Pangolin: Still, one has to wonder if the Great Disaster really intended to harm them.
Pangolin: If a monsteratops truly wanted to squash you, would you be able to outrun its feet?
